# PROPOSAL — NOT APPLIED. DO NOT auto-apply / auto-deploy. Orchestrator deploys after owner review.
#
# WHAT: Add FIVE new NOTIFICATION-ONLY automations (ids 1789500001001..1789500001005)
# that watch the Proxmox `mylab` infrastructure and alert on Telegram. They issue
# ZERO device commands — the ONLY action anywhere is telegram_bot.send_message
# (chat_id 100000000). No Proxmox button/switch/service, no climate/script/
# input_boolean, no `target:`. Verified by tests/test_proxmox_watchdog.py.
#
# CONDITIONS MODELED (each: DOWN alert with a `for:` debounce + a RECOVERY message):
#   1789500001001  NODE OFFLINE      binary_sensor.mylab_status -> off/unavailable/
#                                    unknown for 5m; recovery on -> for 2m.
#   1789500001002  VM STOPPED        the 5 RUNNING non-template VM status
#                                    binary_sensors on->off for 2m; recovery off->on.
#                                    Template/base vmid 9000
#                                    (binary_sensor.homelab_clean_ubuntu_2404_status)
#                                    is EXCLUDED — a stopped template is NOT an alert.
#   1789500001003  STORAGE CRITICAL  active storage %used sensors (nas,
#                                    local-btrfs) >= 90% for 10m; recovery < 90% for
#                                    5m. Inactive `local` (usage unknown) excluded.
#                                    This is DISK, not RAM.
#   1789500001004  BACKUP ERR/STALE  binary_sensor.mylab_backup_status (problem) = on
#                                    for 10m, OR sensor.mylab_last_backup age > 36h
#                                    (checked twice daily 09:00/21:00 to avoid spam);
#                                    recovery on problem-clear and on a fresh backup
#                                    landing after a >36h gap.
#   1789500001005  STALE METRIC      sensor.mylab_cpu_usage unavailable/unknown for
#                                    30m OR last_updated age > 30m (reminder throttled
#                                    to <= 1/3h); recovery when it updates again.
#
# RAM-PRESSURE ALERTING — DEFERRED (owner rule): NO RAM%-threshold alert is created.
#   Proxmox mem% (e.g. homelab-dev 98.6%, mylab 80.9%) counts Linux page cache and is
#   NOT a pressure signal. The inventory (metrics_available) exposes NO OOM, NO swap,
#   NO PSI/pressure, NO load sensor (`node_load: false`), and a grep of the 159
#   entities finds none. Alerting on RAM% alone would be a chronic false alarm, so it
#   is intentionally omitted until a real OOM/swap/pressure signal exists. If such a
#   signal appears later, add a separate conservative watchdog then.
#
# COOLDOWN / ANTI-SPAM: mode: single on every automation; `for:` debounce on both
#   the DOWN and RECOVERY edges of the state-based conditions so WiFi/poll flaps do
#   not page; the two time-driven checks (backup staleness, metric staleness) are
#   rate-limited (twice-daily fixed times / a last_triggered 3h gate).
#
# CHOSEN IDS + UNIQUENESS: 1789500001001, ...002, ...003, ...004, ...005 — all
#   verified UNIQUE against the read-only fetched /config/automations.yaml (53
#   existing automation blocks, no collision). The patch is purely additive: it
#   appends 5 blocks at end of file; NO existing automation is modified (the only
#   "-" line is the file's last context line `  max: 20`, re-added verbatim because
#   the file has no trailing newline — a pure diff artifact).
#
# TARGET FILE ON HA: /config/automations.yaml (fetched read-only via `sudo cat`,
#   190764 bytes, 4355 lines, 53 automations, NO trailing newline — hence the
#   "\ No newline at end of file" marker on the last unchanged context line).
#
# APPLY (after review, from a dir containing the fetched automations.yaml):
#   patch -p1 --dry-run < docs/audit/proxmox_watchdog.patch   # verify
#   patch -p1          < docs/audit/proxmox_watchdog.patch     # apply to working copy
#   # then, on HA: back up first, sanity-check, deploy, reload:
#   #   sudo cp /config/automations.yaml /config/automations.yaml.bak_proxmox_$(date +%s)
#   #   (copy patched file to /config/automations.yaml)
#   #   POST /api/config/core/check_config      # sanity BEFORE reload
#   #   POST /api/services/automation/reload
#
# ROLLBACK:
#   patch -p1 -R < docs/audit/proxmox_watchdog.patch           # revert working copy
#   # on HA: restore the .bak_proxmox_* backup to /config/automations.yaml, then
#   #   POST /api/services/automation/reload
#   # OR simply delete automation ids 1789500001001..005 (all additive & device-inert).
#
